{'questions': ["Consider a discrete-time linear dynamical system described by the state transition matrix $A = \begin{pmatrix} 0.8 & 0.3 \\ 0.2 & 0.7 \end{pmatrix}$. This matrix is used for studying the system's stability.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Compute all eigenvectors of the matrix $A$.
    \item Let $P$ be the matrix whose columns are the computed eigenvectors, and $D$ be the diagonal matrix containing the corresponding eigenvalues. Verify the spectral decomposition accuracy by showing that $AP = PD$.
\end{enumerate}", 'A control engineer is analyzing the long-term behavior of a discrete-time control system, whose dynamics are governed by the matrix $B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0.5 \end{pmatrix}$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Determine all eigenvectors of the matrix $B$.
    \item Construct the matrix $P$ from the eigenvectors and the diagonal matrix $D$ from the eigenvalues. Verify the spectral decomposition accuracy by computing $PDP^{-1}$ and demonstrating that it equals the original matrix $B$.
\end{enumerate}']}

{'questions': ["Consider a new infectious disease, 'A', which affects \(1\%\) of the general population. A diagnostic test for disease A has a sensitivity of \(95\%\) and a specificity of \(90\%\). An individual from this population takes the test and receives a positive result. Explain, without performing a full numerical calculation, how the positive test result updates the prior belief about the individual's probability of having disease A. Specifically, discuss the roles of the prior prevalence of the disease and the test's accuracy (sensitivity and specificity) in shaping the posterior probability.", "A very rare neurological disorder, 'N', is estimated to affect \(0.05\%\) of the population. A new diagnostic test for disorder N boasts a sensitivity of \(98\%\) and a specificity of \(99\%\). An individual from the general population undergoes this test and receives a positive result. Explain how Bayes' Theorem allows us to update the prior probability of having disorder N into a posterior probability after observing the positive test result. In your explanation, specifically address why, even with such a seemingly accurate test, the posterior probability of actually having the rare disorder might not be as high as intuitively expected.", "Imagine two different screening tests for 'Disease X', which has a population prevalence of \(2\%\).
\begin{itemize}
    \item Test A: Sensitivity of \(90\%\) and Specificity of \(95\%\).
    \item Test B: Sensitivity of \(98\%\) and Specificity of \(80\%\).
\end{itemize}
An individual tests positive with Test A, and another individual (from the same population) tests positive with Test B. Without performing full numerical calculations for both, explain how the posterior probability of having Disease X for the individual tested with Test A would update their prior beliefs differently compared to the individual tested with Test B. Focus on how the distinct sensitivity and specificity values of each test influence the degree and direction of the belief update.", "A common metabolic condition, 'M', affects \(15\%\) of adults. A preliminary screening test for condition M has a sensitivity of \(85\%\) and a specificity of \(70\%\). An individual takes this test and receives a positive result. Using the principles of conditional probability, describe the factors that contribute to the calculation of the posterior probability (the probability of actually having condition M given a positive test result). Your explanation should detail how the prior probability, the likelihood of a true positive, and the likelihood of a false positive combine to update our initial belief.", "A local health clinic is introducing a new rapid test for 'Virus V', which currently has a prevalence of \(5\%\) in the community. The test developer proudly states the test has a 'detection rate' (sensitivity) of \(99\%\). A patient tests positive for Virus V and states, 'Since the test detects the virus \(99\%\) of the time when it's present, I'm \(99\%\) certain I have the virus.' Explain why this patient's conclusion is incorrect and how the posterior probability of having Virus V after a positive test result must account for more than just the test's sensitivity. In your explanation, highlight the missing information crucial for an accurate assessment and its role in Bayes' Theorem."]}
